# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GROUPS. Partitioned by what someone is trying to CHANGE, not by directory.
#
# Every path must map to exactly one group, and anything unmatched falls to
# `host` - a path that maps to nothing would be a silent hole in the system
# built to prevent silent holes.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GROUPS -> SUBGROUPS -> paths.
#
# Two reasons for the second level, and speed is the one Milos asked for:
#   1. LOCALISATION. "network/dns changed" beats "network changed".
#   2. COST CONTROL. Each subgroup carries its own file budget, so one huge tree
#      (an app checkout with a repo in it) cannot starve the cheap config paths
#      that actually matter. Config subgroups are tiny and always fully checked;
#      bulk subgroups are capped and say so.
#
# A group's digest is the hash of its subgroup digests, so a change is attributed
# to a subgroup without rehashing anything else.
#
# Every path maps to exactly one subgroup. Unmatched falls to host/other, because
# a path mapping to nothing would be a silent hole in the system built to prevent
# silent holes.
